### Seven work orders in tenant north. The shape of this data is what every later assertion is
### measured against, so it is deliberate rather than arbitrary:
###   - priorities 1,1,2,2,3,3,4 — three ties, so keyset paging has to lean on the `id` tie-breaker
###   - WO-1001..WO-1003 assigned to `tech-north`, the rest to nobody: a technician's list is a subset
###   - `scheduled_for` set on two and null on five: `is.null` has a non-trivial answer
###   - every status value used, so `or=(…)`, `not.` and `neq` each exclude a different set
###   - `internal_notes` and `access_code` on every row: both hidden, so neither may ever come back
# @name CreateWo1001
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantNorth}}",
  "reference": "WO-1001",
  "title": "Boiler service",
  "description": "Annual service of the two ground-floor boilers.",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 1,
  "quoted_price": 1250.50,
  "is_emergency": false,
  "scheduled_for": "2026-09-01T08:00:00+00:00",
  "contact_email": "site@acme.test",
  "metadata": { "floor": 3, "keys_at": "reception" },
  "assigned_to": "{{userTechNorth}}",
  "internal_notes": "Customer disputes last year's invoice.",
  "access_code": "ACME-1001",
  "customer_id": "{{CustomerAcmeId}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

